#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:11
msgid "Documentation guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:14
msgid ""
"Consistent documentation helps contributors create ROS articles that are "
"easy to review, maintain, and navigate. This article describes the RST "
"formatting conventions, metadata, directives, content types, and other "
"guidelines used when creating ROS documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:23
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:26
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:28
msgid ""
"When creating content for the ROS documentation, write in reStructuredText "
"(RST) and ensure that you follow good practice guidelines, or your pull "
"request may not be accepted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:31
msgid ""
"This article relates to contributing to the ROS documentation site. For more "
"information about creating or updating package documentation, "
"see :doc:`../../../Developer-Tools/Package-documentation/Documenting-a-ROS-2-"
"Package`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:35
msgid "Formatting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:37
msgid ""
"The ROS documentation website uses the ``reStructuredText`` format, which is "
"the default plaintext markup language used by Sphinx. This section is a "
"brief introduction to ``reStructuredText`` concepts, syntax, and best "
"practices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:40
msgid ""
"When formatting your ``reStructuredText`` file, write only one sentence per "
"line. That makes reviewing and modifying your file much easier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:43
msgid ""
"You can refer to `reStructuredText User Documentation <https://"
"docutils.sourceforge.io/rst.html>`_ for a detailed technical specification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:46
msgid "Whitespace"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:48
msgid ""
"The ROS documentation linter will not accept pull requests with trailing "
"white space. We recommend that you enable automatic white space highlighting "
"and cleanup if your editor supports it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:51
msgid "You must also include a single newline at the end of the file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:54
msgid "Headings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:56
msgid ""
"There are four main heading types used in the documentation. Note that the "
"number of underline characters must match the length of the title."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:59
msgid ""
"Page title heading\n"
"==================\n"
"\n"
"Section heading\n"
"---------------\n"
"\n"
"2 Subsection heading\n"
"^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n"
"\n"
"2.4 Subsubsection heading\n"
"~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:73
msgid ""
"We usually use one digit for numbering subsections and two digits (dot "
"separated) for numbering subsubsections in tutorials and how-to guides."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:76
msgid "Lists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:78
msgid ""
"Stars ``*`` are used for listing unordered items using bullet points, and "
"the number sign ``#.`` is used for listing numbered items. Both of them "
"support nested definitions and will render accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:81
msgid ""
"* Bullet point\n"
"\n"
"  * Bullet point nested\n"
"  * Bullet point nested\n"
"\n"
"* Bullet point"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:90
msgid ""
"#. First listed item\n"
"#. Second listed item"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:96
msgid "Code formatting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:98
#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:100
msgid ""
"In-text code can be formatted using ``backticks`` for showing "
"``highlighted`` code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:104
msgid ""
"Code blocks inside a page need to be captured using the ``.. code-block::`` "
"`directive <https://www.sphinx-doc.org/en/master/usage/restructuredtext/"
"directives.html#directive-code-block>`_. ``.. code-block::`` supports code "
"highlighting for syntaxes like ``C++``, ``YAML``, ``console``, ``bash``, and "
"more. Code inside the directive needs to be indented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:108
msgid ""
".. code-block:: C++\n"
"\n"
"   int main(int argc, char** argv)\n"
"   {\n"
"      rclcpp::init(argc, argv);\n"
"      rclcpp::spin(std::make_shared<ParametersClass>());\n"
"      rclcpp::shutdown();\n"
"      return 0;\n"
"   }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:121
msgid "Code blocks: ``bash`` vs. ``console``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:123
msgid ""
"``bash`` and ``console`` are similar, but they serve two different purposes. "
"Choosing the correct one ensures that the content is formatted correctly and "
"that the copy button copies the right content."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:126
msgid ""
"``bash`` is meant for scripts, for example for bash commands from a script "
"file. The entire code block is copied when the copy button is selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:128
msgid ""
"``console`` is meant for commands to be run in a terminal, optionally "
"including their output. Only lines with a prompt symbol are copied to the "
"clipboard when the copy button is selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:132
msgid "bash"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:134
msgid "Use ``.. code-block:: bash`` without ``$``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:136
msgid ""
"export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42\n"
"ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:141
msgid ""
"``bash`` can be used without ``$`` for commands meant to be run in a "
"terminal if the code block does not include any output lines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:144
msgid "console"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:146
msgid ""
"We recommend using ``.. code-block:: console`` with ``$`` on all command "
"lines for consistency and clarity. This makes it clear that the given "
"commands need to be run in a terminal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:149
msgid ""
"``console`` separates command lines from output lines using prompt symbols "
"such as ``$`` or ``#``. Lines that begin with a prompt symbol are formatted "
"as bash commands. Other lines are formatted as normal text. The prompt "
"symbol cannot be selected in the browser. The copy button in the upper right-"
"hand corner copies *only* the commands, not the outputs or the prompt "
"symbols. If a ``console`` code block does not have any lines with a prompt "
"symbol, the copy button does not copy any lines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:156
msgid ""
"If there is output that needs to be displayed, include it in the same block:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:158
msgid ""
"$ source /opt/ros/rolling/setup.bash\n"
"$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node\n"
"[INFO] [1743878028.269334696] [turtlesim]: Starting turtlesim with node "
"name /turtlesim\n"
"[INFO] [1743878028.275096618] [turtlesim]: Spawning turtle [turtle1] at "
"x=[5.544445], y=[5.544445], theta=[0.000000]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:167
msgid ""
"If some output lines start with ``#``, place the output in a separate ``.. "
"code-block:: text``. ``#`` is treated as a prompt, like ``$``, so those "
"lines would otherwise be copied as commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:171
msgid "Bash and console examples"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:173
msgid ""
"You can compare the text that is copied to your clipboard when you select "
"the copy button for the two different types of code blocks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:175
msgid "The following code block is ``console``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:177
#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:188
msgid ""
"$ export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42\n"
"$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node --ros-args --remap "
"\"__node:=my_turtle\"\n"
"[INFO] [1742150439.022947971] [my_turtle]: Starting turtlesim with node "
"name /my_turtle\n"
"[INFO] [1742150439.026043867] [my_turtle]: Spawning turtle [turtle1] at "
"x=[5.544445], y=[5.544445], theta=[0.000000]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:184
msgid ""
"The following code block uses ``bash`` with ``$`` prompts and output lines. "
"This is **incorrect**: the copy button copies everything, including the "
"prompts and the output. It is shown here only for comparison."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:196
msgid "Images"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:198
msgid ""
"Images can be inserted in an article by using the ``.. image::`` directive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:200
msgid ".. image:: images/turtlesim_follow1.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:204
msgid ""
"In this case, the image file (``turtlesim_follow1.png``) is located in the "
"``images/`` directory relative to the ``.rst`` file that uses the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:206
msgid ""
"However, all image files end up in an ``_images/`` directory relative to the "
"root of the docs. Therefore, when using ``:target:`` to add a hyperlink to "
"the image file, use a relative link going up to the root directory and then "
"down to the ``_images/`` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:209
msgid ""
".. image:: images/turtlesim_follow1.png\n"
"   :target: ../../_images/turtlesim_follow1.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:215
msgid "Charts, graphs, and diagrams"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/The-ROS2-Project/Contributing/Documentation/Documentation-guidelines.rst:217
msgid ""
"ROS documentation supports charts, graphs, and diagrams written using "
"`Mermaid Charts <https://mermaid.js.org/intro/>`__. Prefer Mermaid instead "
